[h1]Deep Frontier[/h1]
[b]Ever feel like your endgame wonder should be the start of the game, not the end?[/b] Deep Frontier picks up where the Iron Teeth usually stop: dig into the deep ore below your map, pour concrete, smelt steel, run coal, oil and nuclear power, keep your beavers fed and healthy, and push all the way to a rocket launch, while radiation, smog and workplace injuries try to send you back to the stone age.

[b]64 new buildings, 55 goods and 75 recipes.[/b]

[i]Iron Teeth only. Timberborn 1.1.2.8 or later. Playable and actively developed: expect balance changes between versions.[/i]

[h2]What's in it[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Deep ore:[/b] Deep Drills and Deep Mines reach finite deposits and permanent veins below the surface; the Deep Ore Processor turns the ore into coal, crude oil, uranium and alloy.
[*][b]Construction materials:[/b] sandstone and limestone quarries and pits, stone crusher, cement kiln and concrete mixer; concrete and masonry blocks, scaffolds, side platforms, overhangs and reinforced dams.
[*][b]Heavy industry:[/b] steel mill, chemical works, precision machine works, electrolysis and cryogenic liquefaction plants, water treatment.
[*][b]Power:[/b] reinforced hydro turbine; coal (5,000 hp) and oil (20,000 hp) power plants that run without workers and burn fuel only while your network needs it; and a nuclear reactor that can melt down if you neglect it.
[*][b]Food:[/b] fishery works, fish hatchery, greenhouse (with aquaponics), fertilizer works and food packaging.
[*][b]Civic life:[/b] a restaurant with real table service, where a full meal earns the Fine Dining bonus; a grocery store; a hospital and public health lab; a bathhouse, town plaza and observation deck.
[*][b]Smog:[/b] industry pollutes the air around it; plant trees or build smog scrubbers.
[*][b]Workforce:[/b] dangerous jobs can injure beavers and bots need upkeep. Key buildings need trained specialists: every beaver studies at a Technical School in its free time, and a beaver can learn every profession. A specialist's skills die with them.
[*][b]Science and the rocket program:[/b] materials laboratory, archive excavation, rocket assembly hangar, mission control and launch pad, with missions to launch.
[/list]

[h2]Radioactive Tides and radioactive waste[/h2]
Radiation is not a reskinned badtide. It is worse, and it lingers.
[list]
[*][b]Radioactive Tides[/b] are a hazardous season in which your water sources pour out radioactive water. They start after 15 cycles at the earliest, and you can change that or switch them off in the mod settings.
[*]Radiation [b]kills crops the moment it reaches them[/b].
[*]When radioactive water dries out on your fields, [b]the ground stays irradiated[/b] until you rinse it clean.
[*][b]Pumps cannot filter radiation[/b]: they shut off entirely while they detect any.
[*]Automation built for badtides will not save you. Set your Weather Stations to their Radioactive Tide mode, and use contamination sensors that measure radiation.
[*]Irradiated beavers [b]do not recover on their own[/b]. They need a hospital and its medicine, which takes a hefty supply chain.
[*]Industry produces radioactive waste that you must refine, lock in a vault or ship off the map.
[/list]
There is more to discover; play to find it.

[h2]Plan your logistics[/h2]
Many buildings consume and produce far more than haulers can keep up with. [b]Pipes and conveyor belts are recommended, and for some buildings essential.[/b] Plan your layout accordingly!

[h2]The long game[/h2]
You will need a [b]ton[/b] of science. The nuclear reactor alone costs [b]1,000,000 science[/b], plus trained reactor operators and a uranium supply chain, but once it is running, you will never run short of power again.

[h2]Quality of life[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Building passages:[/b] buildings that touch share their insides, stacked or side by side, so beavers walk through one building to reach the next, much like the Hooman Stairs mod.
[*][b]Pump to Tank[/b] is a required mod. Use it: otherwise half your colony will be carrying water by the late game.
[*]Deep Frontier's buildings sit in their own toolbar sub-groups (deep mining, heavy industry, rocketry and more), so the native menus stay tidy.
[/list]

[h2]Maps[/h2]
Start a new game on any built-in map and Deep Frontier places its resources for you: deep ore veins and sources, limestone and sandstone outcrops, a limestone seam and Human Record Caches. The build height rises to 66 layers so the tall late-game buildings fit. Deep Frontier versions of Plains, Plains Hard, Lakes and Terraces are included as map files too. You can also place the new resources yourself in the map editor. Existing Iron Teeth saves work, but they don't get the new resources.

[b]Small maps are EXTREMELY difficult with this mod.[/b] Diorama and Cliffside have no room for the Nuclear Reactor, the Launch Pad and much of the late game; pick a large map for a full playthrough. Some larger maps also lack a site for one or two late-game buildings; the game tells you which when you start.

[h2]Required mods[/h2]
Subscribe to these too (Steam lists them under Required items):
[list]
[*]Harmony
[*]Moddable Timberborn
[*]Moddable Tool Groups
[*]Mod Settings (eMka)
[*]TimberUi
[*]Moddable Recipes
[*]New Styles
[*]Pump to Tank
[*]Flywheels
[*]Teleporter Station
[*]Timber Pipes
[*]Solar Panel (northforge)
[*]Expanded Storage
[*]Conveyor Belts
[*]Powered Tubeway
[*]Power Lines
[*]Map Transformer
[/list]
Optional: Moddable Weathers (for the Radioactive Tide weather) and Sluice is Back! (radiation-aware sluices).

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Not compatible with Hooman Stairs.[/b] Deep Frontier links touching buildings itself. While Hooman Stairs is enabled, Deep Frontier turns its building passages off and says so on the main menu.
[/list]

[h2]Feedback[/h2]
Please report bugs in the comments with your game log (Documents\Timberborn\Logs) and, if you can, a save.
